@page "/health-checks"
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Monthly computer health check reports";
    ViewData["Description"] = "A monthly health check of each client's computers: disk health, backups, updates, security and Windows 11 readiness, in one report per client.";
}
<section class="page-head">
    <div class="wrap">
        <h1>Monthly health checks for your clients' computers</h1>
        <p class="lede">Once a month, each computer checks itself and does its own housekeeping. Your client gets one report showing every
            computer you look after for them — and you see trouble before it becomes a call.</p>
        <div class="next-links"><partial name="_SiteCta" /></div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
        <h2>What it checks</h2>
        <ul class="feature-list" style="margin-top:18px">
            <li>Disk health, read against the maker's own thresholds</li>
            <li>Disk space, and what is filling it</li>
            <li>Backups: is there one, is it recent, can it be read</li>
            <li>Windows updates</li>
            <li>Security: antivirus, firewall, encryption</li>
            <li>Crashes and unexpected shutdowns</li>
            <li>Windows activation and the Office version</li>
            <li>Windows 11 readiness: a free upgrade, or a hardware quote</li>
            <li>The computer's age and support status</li>
            <li>Windows system files</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="muted" style="margin-top:14px">It never calls a computer healthy without the evidence to say so.</p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section alt">
    <div class="wrap grid2">
        <div>
            <h2>And the housekeeping</h2>
            <p>Temporary files and caches cleared, a virus scan, drives optimised and system files checked — after a restore point is made,
                so there is always a way back. The recycle bin is never emptied for them; the report says what is in it.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
            <h2>For the clients you choose</h2>
            <p>Tick <em>Monthly health check</em> on a client and every computer of theirs follows — for your SLA clients, say — or turn it on
                or off for one computer. Our service on the computer installs and removes it; nobody visits.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
        <h2>A report your client reads</h2>
        <p>One report per client, a section per computer, emailed from your own address with your own logo. It shows them, every month, the
            work they pay you for — and the upgrades they will need.</p>
        <div class="next-links"><a class="btn" href="/remote-support">Remote support</a> <a class="btn" href="/pricing">Pricing</a></div>
    </div>
</section>
